// ?Rva00396D40DoSetRallyPoint@@YA_NPAVObject@@ABUCoord3D@@_N0@Z
// partial score=0.96 date=2026-09-28
// cl: /DNDEBUG /MD /EHsc /Igame/Libraries/Source/WWVegas/WWLib
//
// Retail 0x00396D40, the rally-point setter the game reaches through the ILT
// thunk 0x00001479.  The Zero Hour twin is the file-static
// GameLogicDispatch.cpp:doSetRallyPoint and every string this body quotes is
// that function's ("HumanLocomotor", "GUI:RallyPointNoPath",
// "UnableToSetRallyPoint", "GUI:RallyPointSet", "RallyPointSet"), so the
// identity is the twin's.  The four-argument shape and the Bool result are not
// the twin's, so the ledger name keeps the address token.
//
// Both retail callers fix the extra arguments.  GameLogic's message dispatcher
// (0x00397540 +0x05EB) pushes (object, &coord, 1, 0) and the resolve callback
// bfmeResolveReferenceIfEnabled (0x003972F0 +0x26) pushes (object, &coord, 0, 0),
// so the third argument is the per-object feedback switch - the global-rally-point
// pass in Rva00397350GlobalRallyPointFeedback.cpp runs with it clear and prints
// one aggregate message instead - and the fourth is an optional object whose
// position replaces the requested one.  Both callers pass zero for the fourth,
// so its class is unproven; only its +0x38 Coord3D is read.
//
// The four-argument reading is re-proved from the callers, and the frame
// arithmetic here is easy to get wrong: the prologue's `push -1` counts.  At
// 0x00397B2B the dispatcher pushes 0, 1, &coord, object and then `add
// esp,0x10`, so cdecl arg1 IS the object; the body agrees, because after
// `push -1; push handler; push fs:[0]; sub esp,0x34; push ebx/esi/edi` the
// return address sits at [esp+0x48] and the object at [esp+0x50] - which is
// the [esp+0x4c] this build reads one push earlier, since it has only pushed
// ebx and esi by then.  [esp+0x4c] is the saved SEH link, not an argument.
//
// STATE 2026-09-27 (second pass): 37 non-relocation bytes differ from retail, all
// of them two residues, and the compiled size is retail's 880 exactly.  Both
// residues are codegen order, not identity: every value keeps retail's role, so
// the reconstruction is right and only MSVC's two choices are wrong.
//   1. An ESI/EDI mirror, at four sites (+0x1b, +0x54, +0x98, +0x1b9) and their
//      users.  Every value keeps retail's role: the object pointer is the
//      long-lived one, the NameKey result, the &Object::m_position and the
//      Drawable are the short-lived scratch, and isLocal is in EBX.  Retail
//      gives the long-lived web EDI and the scratch ESI, this build the other
//      way round.
//   2. The 12-byte copy at the top.  Retail emits store 1, then the NameKey
//      call's argument setup, then stores 2 and 3, with the two later
//      displacements already adjusted for the push - so this is codegen order,
//      not a scheduled move.  MSVC stores all three before the call setup here.
//
// MEASURED THIS PASS, all negative, all at 37 bytes / 880, object still in ESI:
//   * The choice is set by the PATHFINDER block, and by nothing later.  With
//     only the head (isLocal, the copy, nameToKey, the LocomotorSet) the object
//     gets NO callee-saved register at all - it is reloaded from the argument
//     slot on every use.  Adding the block that uses the object twice and takes
//     &object->m_position is what forces the allocation.  Deleting the whole
//     tail (final feedback block emptied: 503 B; first static removed: 743 B)
//     leaves head=esi, so the "something later in this function" lead is dead.
//   * Making Object::getPosition() an out-of-line CALL does NOT flip it (897 B,
//     head=esi).  The flip is caused by the object's USE COUNT, not by the
//     inline accessor's lea, so do not re-run the reduced-TU experiment.
//   * Hoisting the position to a local before the first statement does not flip
//     it either way: a `const Coord3D &` reference and a `const Coord3D *`
//     pointer both cost 7 bytes (873 B) and add a fourth save (EBP), because the
//     value is not live at the top so MSVC sinks its materialisation below the
//     isLocallyControlled call.  The shape_levers.md "a field address counts
//     before a field load" lever needs the address to fold into the addressing
//     mode, which it cannot here.
//   * &object->m_position instead of &object->getPosition(), a pointer-returning
//     getPositionPtr(), pointer->reference callee parameters, a top-level const
//     object parameter, block-scoping the NameKey key, declaring the
//     LocomotorSet before the key, a named local for findLocomotorTemplate's
//     result, a cached NameKeyGenerator* receiver, and declared-then-assigned
//     rallyPosition all leave 37 bytes / head=esi.
//   * shape_family_levers.py reports NO applicable source-level family lever for
//     this source, and shape_search over eh_levers.py's six mechanical choices
//     (throw() on three callees, /EHsc-, _STLP_NO_EXCEPTIONS, nothrow delete[])
//     scores the unchanged source best.  rotation_sweep.py finds 16 toggles, all
//     +0.  This matches the documented limit: that lever does not reach
//     callee-saved swaps, and docs/shape_levers.md records bodies where the
//     choice stays compiler-internal after every choice is exhausted.
//
// STATE 2026-09-27 (third pass): still 880 B with the same 37 bytes, still
// head=esi and the copy still flat.  New negative results, all at 880/37 with
// head=esi unless stated:
//   * Residue 2 spellings, none of which move the store order: Coord3D as a
//     standalone three-float struct instead of deriving from Vector3, the copy
//     through an explicit Vector3::operator=, three separate int-punned dword
//     assignments, the same through int pointers, a copy constructor taking a
//     reference, comma-expression sequencing, declaration plus assignment, and
//     a copy off a pointer local.  Writing the three dword moves as three
//     statements rather than one aggregate ASSIGN does NOT interleave them
//     either, so "aggregate assign vs statement sequence" is not the lever.
//   * The only spelling found that DOES make MSVC interleave the copy with
//     nameToKey's setup is a three-argument Coord3D constructor (880 B, 49
//     diffs): the moves then go through x87 fld/fstp and the stores land as
//     y, x, then z around the push, so the interleave is reachable but only
//     with the wrong instructions and the wrong order.  memcpy of the
//     aggregate ICEs cl 13.10.3077, so that spelling does not exist here.
//   * The path-test polarity is already the right one: making the copy the
//     then-arm (`if (positionSource) {...} else if (pathtest) {...}`) is
//     895 B / 584 diffs.  Hoisting the two function-local statics to function
//     scope is 887 B; hoisting the Drawable out of the feedback block is 880 B
//     / 37.
//   * CORRECTION to the first pass: "a reduced TU reproduces retail's order
//     exactly" does not hold for the bisection rungs kept in build/var.  With
//     only the head, with the head plus the pathfinder block, and with those
//     plus the ExitInterface block, this build is head=esi AND flat every time.
//     Do not re-run the reduced-TU experiment from that note.
//   * Both residues now match the documented compiler-internal limit on this
//     toolchain (MSVC 13.10.3077, docs/callee_visibility.md): shape_levers.md
//     says the rotation lever does not reach callee-saved swaps, and
//     docs/sib_lea_experiments.md leaves a different c2.dll build as the
//     remaining hypothesis for an allocation-only residue of exactly this
//     shape.  Next worker should not spend a pass on spelling variants.
// CORRECTION to the previous STATE note: landing does NOT need pins for
// 0x012F0C70 / 0x012F0BE8.  Those are the addresses RETAIL's linker gave the two
// function-local statics; in this object both the statics and their guard bytes
// are IMAGE_REL_I386_DIR32 relocations onto ?rallyNotSet@?M@... and
// ?rallyPointSet@?BM@... (offsets 259, 272, 304, 356, 374 for the first, 645,
// 663, 695, 746, 751, 767, 772, 785 for the second), so probe masks them and
// the link resolves them wherever they land.  What a landing still has to pass
// is build.sh's DIR32-addresses check, which is a separate gate.
//
// STATE 2026-09-27 (fourth pass): unchanged at 880 B / 37 non-reloc bytes,
// head=esi and the copy still flat after 13 more builds.  This pass stopped
// guessing spellings and went to the MATCHED corpus for a witness of each
// residue instead, so the negative results below are corpus-backed.
//   * Residue 2 (the split copy) is NOT rare in retail.  build/var/scan_rate.py
//     over 40k matched rows finds 16 three-dword copies that feed a call, 11 of
//     them SPLIT around the call's argument setup with the later displacements
//     adjusted for the push - this body's exact shape, retail included.  The
//     nearest witness is 0x000CDEC0 ?bfmeMakeZP@BfmeTail926C@@QAEPAXPAUBfmeTripleZP@@@Z,
//     67 bytes of hand-written C++ in game/GameEngine/Source/Common/BfmeConv1817.cpp
//     whose copy is split around the next call.  Its source spells the copy as
//     THREE SEPARATE NAMED MEMBER ASSIGNMENTS into a bare local struct
//     (local.m_bfmeAZP = source->m_bfmeAZP; ...), and it copies from a pointer
//     parameter exactly as this body does.  That spelling is flat here
//     (V1_threefields), as is the same thing through a pointer local
//     (V2_threeptr), with the three assignments permuted (V3_order*, 42-45 bytes)
//     and with a declared Coord3D ctor (V4_ctortype).  So the witness construct
//     does not transfer, and the remaining 10 split witnesses differ from it in
//     ways that cannot be spelled here without changing the emitted bytes.
//   * Residue 1 (the ESI/EDI mirror) is a TIE, not a rule we are violating.
//     build/var/prologue2.py over 4k matched bodies: where a body's first
//     post-prologue `mov reg,[esp+N]` follows the whole push sequence, it lands
//     in the LAST register pushed in 274 of 275 cases (edi for push ebx,esi,edi
//     24:1, edi for push esi,edi 77:1, esi for push ebx,esi 35:0), so retail's
//     edi is the norm and ours is the exception.  But the two competing
//     variables here are indistinguishable to the allocator: the object pointer
//     and the coalesced key/&m_position/drawable temp each have 7 uses and
//     overlapping ranges in BOTH builds, and the prologue copy sits right after
//     the push of its own register in both, so nothing in the source decides
//     which of them is allocated first.  prologue3.py: a copy emitted BETWEEN two
//     prologue pushes is itself common in the corpus (609 bodies), it simply
//     always targets the register just pushed - which our build does.
//   * Trees, not statements, are not the lever either: the path test spelled as
//     nested ifs (X1_nestedif), as a positive `A || B` with an empty then
//     (X3_positive), and with the three-field copy on top of the nested tree
//     (X2_nested3fld) all emit the same 880 B / 37 bytes, head=esi, r2=flat.
//     Nor is the parameter spelling: the object and the position as REFERENCES
//     (H2_refobj) or the position as a POINTER - which both retail callers push
//     identically, so it is undetectable from the caller side - (H1_ptrarg),
//     and a pointer initializer for the copy (H4_ptrinit) are all 880/37 flat.
//   * Re-ran the mechanical levers as the method requires: eh_levers.py offers 6
//     choices, shape_search scores the UNCHANGED source best (4 trials, no
//     improving output), and shape_family_levers.py still reports "no applicable
//     source-level family lever" for this source.
//   * Verdict after four passes and ~110 builds: the reconstruction and the
//     identity are correct and the only obstacle is MSVC 13.10.3077's scheduler
//     and its register tie-break.  Do not spend another pass on source
//     spellings; the remaining hypothesis is a different c2.dll, which
//     docs/sib_lea_experiments.md already records for an allocation-only residue
//     of this shape.
//
// STATE 2026-09-28 (fifth pass): unchanged at 880 B / 37 non-reloc bytes,
// head=esi, r2=flat, after 8 more builds.  build/var/aln.py aligns the two
// whole instruction streams and settles what is left: 253 instructions each,
// and after neutralising immediates the ONLY differences in the entire body
// are the two residues - a global esi<->edi renumbering and the +0x38 store
// split.  Two consequences, one of them a correction:
//   * The prologue placement is NOT a third residue.  The copy is emitted
//     right after the push of its OWN register in both builds, so ours
//     (push ebx, push esi, mov esi,[arg], push edi) and retail's (push ebx,
//     push esi, push edi, mov edi,[arg]) are the same rule applied to the
//     two different handouts.  Fixing the handout fixes the prologue; there
//     is nothing separate to chase there.
//   * The frame is exactly full: sub esp,0x34 = 12 (rallyPosition) + 32
//     (locomotorSet) + 4 + 4 (the two UnicodeStrings) and not one byte of
//     slack, so the local SET is confirmed and no source variant can add a
//     dword without changing the size.
// This pass deliberately avoided the spelling axis the first four passes
// exhausted and went after the two orders underneath it:
//   * Residue 2 does not depend on the copy's FRONT-END SHAPE at all.
//     build/var/scan_split3.py finds three exact-shape split copies in the
//     matched corpus; two are naked lifts, and the only one with real
//     hand-written C++ is 0x004BFFE0 ?LeftHUDInput@@YAHW4WindowMsgHandledType
//     @@PAVGameWindow@@III@Z in
//     game/GameEngine/Source/GameClient/GUI/GUICallbacks/ControlBarCallback.cpp,
//     whose copy is `info.m_position = world;` - a MEMBER assignment into a
//     struct local, not a whole-aggregate assign into a bare Coord3D.  That
//     construct transfers here BYTE-NEUTRALLY: a single-member struct wrapper
//     (E1_memberbox) and two levels of it (E2_deepbox) leave every address
//     and displacement in the body identical - same 880 B, same 37 bytes -
//     and are still flat, as is the same shape fed from a pointer local
//     (E3_srcptr).  So the split is a function of the surrounding context,
//     not of the copy node: MSVC interleaves it here and not there for
//     reasons no local spelling decides.
//   * Residue 1 is not decided by CREATION ORDER either.  In ours the
//     handout is EBX=isLocal, ESI=object, EDI=scratch; retail's is
//     EBX=isLocal, ESI=scratch, EDI=object, so the question is only which
//     competing value is handed a register first, and that follows the order
//     the front end created the locals.  Declaring each competitor at the
//     top of the function and assigning it at its original place is
//     byte-neutral, and none of it moves the handout: the nameToKey result
//     (F1_keytop), the drawable - the longest-lived scratch besides the
//     object (F2_drawtop), both (F3_both) and the head's own declaration
//     order (D3_declfirst) all give byte-for-byte the same 880/37 head=esi
//     body.  Changing PROGRAM order does change the handout, and only for
//     the worse: the copy before the isLocal call (D2_copypost), the
//     LocomotorSet and key before the head (D1_setfirst) and isLocal after
//     the copy (D5_islocalafter) all cost 59-641 bytes.
//   * Putting the object behind an explicit local (D4_objlocal) costs 54
//     bytes and does not move the handout, so the incoming-argument temp is
//     not what is losing the register either.
// Verdict after five passes and ~125 builds: the reconstruction and the
// identity are correct and both residues are internal to MSVC 13.10.3077's
// scheduler and register tie-break.  The next pass should not try source
// spellings, front-end shapes, or declaration orders; the remaining
// hypothesis is a different c2.dll, which docs/sib_lea_experiments.md
// already records for an allocation-only residue of this shape.

typedef int Int;
typedef bool Bool;
typedef float Real;
typedef unsigned short WideChar;

// The pathfinder predicates are pinned with Vector3 spelled as their own first
// argument type; Coord3D derives from it so the conversions cost no code.
struct Vector3
{
	Real x;
	Real y;
	Real z;
};

struct Coord3D : public Vector3
{
};

#include "ascii_string.h"

// unicode_string.h declares the default constructor out of line; retail
// inlines it, and the two bodies below need the inlined shape.
class UnicodeString
{
public:
	UnicodeString() : m_data(0) {}
	UnicodeString(const UnicodeString &other)
	{
		((StringBase<unsigned short> *)this)->StringBase<unsigned short>::StringBase(
			*(const StringBase<unsigned short> *)&other);
	}
	~UnicodeString()
	{
		((StringBase<unsigned short> *)this)->releaseBuffer();
	}
	void __cdecl format(UnicodeString format, ...);

	// Retail hands the substitution operand the StringBase header pointer, not
	// the +0x08 text its own format() walks (0x00396D16 is a bare
	// `mov eax,[name]`), so this returns the header.
	void *getRawBuffer() const { return m_data; }

private:
	void *m_data;
};

